  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 415 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nSynoptic pattern continues to shift with the ridging to the\nsoutheast pushing eastward over the next day or so. Troughing\nover the northern CONUS will push southeast into the Great \nLakes region and push the band of recent rain that has been in \nplace just to our north and west south through the CWA beginning\ntoday through tomorrow morning in the form of a cold front. A \nfew embedded thunderstorms are possible with this frontal \npassage but lack of any decent shear and instability any storms\nshould remain weak but in any storms heavy downpours will be\npossible especially with PWAT values around 1.75 to 2 inches.\nWPC ERO has our area mainly under a marginal risk for excessive\nrainfall but a small area mostly for the western portions of the\nCWA is under a slight risk for excessive rainfall. \n\nIn the wake of the front on Friday morning into the early \nafternoon cooler temperatures and drier conditions will arrive\nand with a more zonal flow in place aloft shall continue through\nthe weekend into next week. Highs each day mainly in the 60s\nwith a few locations far south getting close to the 70 degree\nmark. Lows will also drop into the 40s each night beginning \nSaturday morning. The coldest morning looks to be Tuesday with \nlows getting into the upper 30s. Time to make sure your furnaces\nare tuned up for the season.",
